10. Manual Review Sample Size for Validation
Primary Sources: GitSkills Population Counts & MSR Open Science Guidelines.
Dataset Context: Population consists of 1,877,981 distinct skill contents.
Methodological Sample Size Calculation:
Statistically Representative Sample: To achieve a 95% Confidence Level with a 5% Margin of Error over a population of 1.88M distinct contents, a random sample of 384 skills is required.
Stratified Sampling Strategy for False Positives / Negatives:
Sample A (Automated Risk Flags - 200 skills): Randomly pull 200 skills flagged by automated scripts as "risky" to measure Precision / False Positive Rate.
Sample B (Unflagged Baseline - 200 skills): Randomly pull 200 skills marked "safe" to measure Recall / False Negative Rate.
Sample C (Cohort Balance): Ensure stratification across copy-count tiers (e.g., 100 singletons, 100 widely reused).
Inter-Rater Agreement Measurement:
Have at least two independent reviewers manually code a shared subset of 100 skills.
Compute Cohen’s Kappa ($\kappa$) (for two raters) or Fleiss’ Kappa (for >2 raters) to report inter-reviewer agreement in the final paper.
 
11. Evaluate whether there are any potential privacy concerns that could arise from use of the dataset.
Primary Sources: GitSkills Anonymisation, Ethics, & License Statements.
Dataset Anonymization Controls Provided:
Commit author accounts were converted to keyed one-way hash codes.
Email addresses and personal names in commit messages were masked.
Evaluated Privacy Risks for the Proposal:
Un-masked Raw File Contents: artifacts.content and artifact_siblings.content reproduce raw files byte-for-byte as published on GitHub. If developers accidentally committed hardcoded credentials (API keys, SSH keys, passwords, bearer tokens) or personal contact information inside SKILL.md or script siblings, those values remain unredacted in the dataset text.
De-Anonymization Risks: Even though commit authors are hashed, repo_full_name links directly to live public GitHub repositories. Cross-referencing repository owners with external data sources could allow researchers to re-identify individual developers.
Compliance Obligation: Researchers must strictly avoid deanonymization, maintain PII masking, and present findings solely in aggregate.
 
12. Consider and present any ethical or security concerns related to the proposal.
Primary Sources: MSR 2027 Mining Challenge Ethics & Licensing Rules.
Ethical & Security Analysis of Proposal:
Dual-Use & Weaponization Risks: The research focuses on identifying "potentially dangerous executable data written into a skill". Publicly highlighting specific widely copied skills containing executable risks or backdoors could act as an operational guide for malicious actors seeking to exploit prompt-injection or supply-chain vulnerabilities in AI coding agents.
Responsible Disclosure: If the automated analysis uncovers active zero-day exploit scripts, live hardcoded secrets, or malicious agent backdoors in public repositories, researchers must follow responsible disclosure protocols rather than publishing raw links or unmasked code.
Shaming & Bias against AI Models/Brands: The proposal intends to evaluate if specific AI models or brands are "targeted and afforded more power". Researchers must ensure rigorous experimental controls so that differences in model security reflect genuine skill capabilities rather than sampling bias (e.g., Claude using .claude/skills/ while generic models lack canonical directories).
Licensing Compliance: Skill text contents remain bound by their original source repository SPDX licenses (repos.license). Any published benchmark datasets or quoted code snippets must respect original open-source license terms.
